 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Short answer:** Women's labour market in Victoria has weakened over the past year. Unemployment is up about a point, while men's has eased slightly.\n\n**Victorian women (seasonally adjusted, September 2026):**\n- **Unemployment rate: 5.1%.** This is up from 4.1% in September 2025 and near the low of 3.9% in April 2025. It peaked at 5.2% in July 2026 and has eased slightly since.\n- **Employed: about 1.84 million.** This is roughly flat year on year (1.838 million in September 2025), after rising through early 2025.\n- **Participation rate: 63.6%.** This is little changed over the year.\n- **Employment-to-population ratio: 60.4%.** This is down from 61.3% in September 2025.\n\n**Comparison with Victorian men (September 2026):**\n- Unemployment is 5.0%, down from 5.3% a year earlier.\n- Participation is 71.5%, so the gap with women is about 8 percentage points.\n\nThe unemployment gap between women and men has nearly closed, with women now slightly higher. The participation gap remains large.\n\nMonthly labour force figures are noisy, so the direction over several months matters more than any single reading.",
